  [1mThe fork:[0m how a form declares its rules. Everything else follows from it, and it's the expensive thing to change —
  switching formats rewrites every form's rules.

  [1m1. Declarative rule schema[0m — rules are data, keyed by name:
  [34mconst[39m signupRules = { [36memail[39m: { [36mrequired[39m: [34mtrue[39m, [36memail[39m: [34mtrue[39m }, [36mpassword[39m: { [36mrequired[39m: [34mtrue[39m, [36mminLength[39m: [32m8[39m } };
  The module owns a registry of rule names and dispatches on them. Buys you the most readable call site and rules that
  are serializable (could come from JSON or a server later). Costs a dispatch table inside the module, and any rule the
  registry doesn't have means editing shared code. Rules out cheap one-off rules.

  [1m2. Composable predicates[0m — rules are functions the module exports and you compose:
  [34mconst[39m signupRules = { [36memail[39m: [[33mrequired[39m(), [33misEmail[39m()], [36mpassword[39m: [[33mrequired[39m(), [33mminLength[39m([32m8[39m)] };
  Buys you a smaller core — no registry, no dispatch, the validator just calls what it's handed — and a form can drop in
  its own inline function without touching the module. Costs slightly noisier call sites and rules that aren't
  serializable. Rules out nothing much.

  [1m3. Shared predicate helpers, no schema[0m — each form hand-writes [38;5;153mvalidateSignup(data)[39m using shared helpers like [38;5;153misEmpty[39m
  / [38;5;153misEmail[39m. Buys maximum flexibility and zero format to get wrong. Costs a hand-written validator per form and no
  enforced error shape — which is most of the duplication you're trying to kill.


